WEBVTT

00:00:00.038 --> 00:00:04.292
Foreign keys also need a decision about what happens when a parent row is deleted.

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:08.767
RESTRICT or NO ACTION blocks the deletion while dependent child rows exist.

00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.812
This is often appropriate for master data such as patients, providers,

00:00:11.812 --> 00:00:15.342
or services that are referenced by historical activity.

00:00:15.342 --> 00:00:18.722
CASCADE automatically removes dependent child rows.

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:22.117
That can be appropriate when the child has no meaning without the parent,

00:00:22.117 --> 00:00:25.632
such as appointment service lines when the appointment itself is deleted.

00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:31.182
SET NULL keeps the child but removes the reference, which only makes sense when the relationship is optional.

00:00:31.182 --> 00:00:34.627
Do not choose CASCADE simply because it is convenient.

00:00:34.627 --> 00:00:39.552
Choose the referential action based on the real meaning and retention requirements of the relationship.

00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:44.727
Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:44.727 --> 00:00:48.907
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:54.652
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:54.652 --> 00:00:59.467
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:04.197
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
